 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Changes from six one-hundredths to five one-hundredths, the portion of a school district's general fund expenditures that a penalty for late final cost reports must exceed for such penalty to be recoverable by the district from the education department.",
 "title": "Changes from six one-hundredths to five one-hundredths, the portion of a school districts general fund expenditures that a penalty for late final cost reports must exceed for such penalty to be recoverable",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A8062",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A8062-2017",
     "printNo": "A8062",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 160\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 3927, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  validate  certain  acts of the Hendrick Hudson central\n      school  district  with  regard  to  certain  capital   improvement\n      projects\"\n \nSenate Bill Number 6779, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to legalize, validate, ratify and confirm the actions of the\n      Panama  central  school  district  notwithstanding  the failure to\n      timely file final building cost reports with the education depart-\n      ment\"\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 8062-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend chapter 54 of  the  laws  of  2016,  directing  the\n      commissioner  of education on how to recover certain penalties, in\n      relation to the collection of a penalty  from  the  Newburgh  city\n      school district\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Each of these bills would authorize, outside of the State Budget proc-\ness,  payment  of  State  education aid above the amounts calculated and\npreviously agreed to under current law.\n \n  These bills would extend or forgive  payback  of  State  building  aid\npayments  for  the  Newburgh  City  School  District, the Panama Central\nSchool District, and the Hendrick Hudson Central School  District,  even\nthough  these  three  districts  failed  to meet long-standing statutory\nrequirements of aid eligibility. Specifically:\n \n  The Newburgh City School District is seeking to extend payback of  its\ncurrently-owed building aid penalty for ten years. However, the District\npreviously  obtained relief, as the District received a five-year exten-\nsion to repay its outstanding penalty as part  of  the  2016-2017  State\nbudget.\n \n  The  Panama  Central School District is seeking forgiveness of certain\nbuilding  aid  penalties.  However,  the  District  previously  obtained\nrelief,  as  it  received  a ten-year extension to repay its outstanding\npenalty as part of the 2013-2014 State budget, and is eligible  to  take\nadvantage  of  extended  building  aid  penalty  payback and forgiveness\nprovisions included in the 2017-18 State budget. Moreover, the  District\nhas  also received financial aid covering the annual cost of its penalty\nrepayment through funding in each of the last four state budgets.\n \n  The Hendrick Hudson Central School District is seeking forgiveness  of\ncertain  building  aid  penalties. The District was granted authority to\nestablish a special tax stabilization reserve fund as part of the  2017-\n18  State  budget  to  help ease future changes to the District's estab-\nlished tax base. Further, I have established a Task Force to address the\nimpact  of Indian Point nuclear power plant's closure, and have included\nan education representative from my office on  that  Task  Force.  I  am\nacutely  concerned with the long-term financial viability of this commu-\nnity after Indian Point closes, and will continue  to  work  with  local\nleaders  and  state legislators to find ways to address the needs of the\nschool district and community, including through  State  budget  negoti-\nations.\n \n  Collectively,  each  of  these  bills  would  result  in increased and\nunbudgeted state costs that will unnecessarily burden  taxpayers.    For\nthese  reasons,  these  bills  must  continue to be addressed within the\ncontext of enacting the overall State budget. I am therefore constrained\nto veto these bills.\n \n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
